I’ll begin with the requested physical inventory: inspect the measurement metadata and pattern, map the chemically plausible phase space, then screen candidate structures and polymorphs before committing to a final GSAS-II refinement. I’ll keep the first profile provisional and restart it cleanly once the phase inventory stabilizes.

The chemistry is a Mn–W–O solid-state reaction at 700 °C, measured with Cu Kα1/Kα2 in reflection geometry. Likely inventory members include one or more manganese tungstates/oxides plus residual WO₃ or Mn₃O₄, but I’ll let diagnostic reflections and physically screened structures decide rather than assume the nominal product.

The first diagnostic match is a wolframite-type MnWO₄ phase (monoclinic P2/c): its low-angle 010/100/011/110 sequence and the split 11̄1/111 pair account for the prominent peaks at about 15.10, 18.06, 23.29/23.80, and 29.58/30.03°. The unusually large apparent low-angle shift is consistent with specimen displacement in reflection geometry, so I’ll refine a single displacement term rather than distort the cell. I’m now screening wolframite parent records and all precursor/secondary-phase alternatives against the same provisional profile.

The physical screen rejects one superficially attractive FeWO₄ record before fitting: after Fe→Mn substitution it gives 1.54 Å W–O and 1.64 Å Mn–O contacts, so its coordinates are not a defensible oxide model. Two independent ordered wolframite parents instead give sensible MnO₆ (2.06–2.26 Å) and WO₆ (1.81–2.16 Å) octahedra. I’m fitting both of those, plus scheelite, fergusonite, MnMoO₄-type, and exact Mn₃WO₆ alternatives with the same provisional background/scale/displacement/cell schedule.

The polymorph screen is decisive: both ordered wolframite parents converge, while scheelite, MnMoO₄-type, and exact Mn₃WO₆ drive their scales to the nonnegative bound. Fergusonite remains nonzero but fits far worse and leaves its diagnostic reflection families unexplained. The secondary-phase screen is now testing room-temperature WO₃ and manganese-oxide polymorphs; early WO₃ branches improve the provisional residual, so residual precursor WO₃ is a serious candidate rather than a background artifact.

The physical inventory is now stable enough for the mandated restart: wolframite-type MnWO₄ plus monoclinic WO₃. The apparent Mn₃O₄ contribution is being treated as a provisional overlap surrogate because its diagnostic peaks miss systematically and its fitted cell expands implausibly. I’m now rebuilding the two-phase refinement from a clean project and will compare common-profile branches from identical starting conditions.

The clean restart exposed a strong cell–specimen-displacement correlation: starting from literature cells makes the least-squares walk very slowly even though the inventory pass already located the relevant basin. I’m seeding only those geometric starting values from the inventory (while keeping all profile/intensity terms reset) so the branch comparison is made after genuine convergence rather than along that transient path.

The common-profile screen is resolving cleanly: every branch containing U drives U through its nonnegative bound and is rejected, while W+X converges without freezing and gives the best accepted common profile so far. I’m finishing the remaining checks to confirm this is not start-value dependent, then I’ll test asymmetry and phase-specific size/strain from that accepted branch.

The phase-by-phase broadening challenge is decisive: MnWO₄ size and strain both run to their neutral bounds and are rejected; WO₃ supports a finite size-only term of about 0.079 µm, lowering Rwp from 9.76% to 9.28%. The WO₃ strain-only alternative is worse and requires an unusually large ~6900 µstrain, so I’m retaining the simpler size interpretation and moving on to polarization and texture challenges after a save/reopen replay.

The intensity tests support a refined effective polarization term, which drops Rwp from 9.28% to 8.03% without bounds or high correlations. Texture is weaker: MnWO₄ [001] gives a stable, modest March–Dollase coefficient (~0.964) and improves several isolated low-, mid-, and high-angle families; WO₃ texture improves the global number but not a consistent set of isolated reflections, so I’m rejecting the WO₃ texture surrogate and retaining only the weak MnWO₄ term.

One physical-inventory issue surfaced before export: the selected WO₃ source CIF encodes all displacement parameters as zero (effectively “not reported”), which is not a credible 300 K model and can distort high-angle intensities. I’m assigning conservative fixed room-temperature values (Uiso(W)=0.0046 Å², Uiso(O)=0.0074 Å², matched to the screened tungstate parent) and, as required when the inventory model changes, restarting the entire final profile sequence from neutral settings.

The clean restart is reproducing the earlier decisions with the corrected WO₃ thermal model: W+X remains the accepted common profile, U again fails at its bound, SH/L remains highly correlated with displacement and unsupported, and both MnWO₄ size and strain again collapse to neutral bounds. This confirms those choices are not artifacts of the original zero-ADP source.

The restarted final model is stable at Rwp ≈ 7.92% with no frozen variables or >0.90 correlations. I also pruned the unsupported eighth background coefficient: seven terms have the better BIC, while six terms measurably degrade the fit. I’m now doing the last model-sensitivity and geometry checks before producing the exact project-matched CIF, profile, JSON, and report.

The model and exports are complete. I’m doing the final cross-file audit now—JSON/CSV integrity, CIF parsing, and exact agreement between `final.gpx`, `fit.csv`, and the reported phase fractions.

